<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Backup is OFF, three ways, because what the app stores is a sign-in: allowBackup for
      Android 11 and below, fullBackupContent for anything that reads the rules instead, and
      dataExtractionRules for Android 12+, where allowBackup alone no longer stops a
      device-to-device transfer. Both rule files exclude every domain (res/xml/).

      usesCleartextTraffic is false: the app is served from https://localhost inside the web
      view (capacitor.config.json, server.androidScheme) and talks to one HTTPS API. A live-reload
      run (cap run -l) serves plain http and would need this lifted locally; never commit that.

      ONE component is exported, because it must be: the launcher activity. The home-screen
      widget's provider is not, and nor are the class countdown's two receivers (each has its
      own note below). There is no custom URL scheme, and no intent filter beyond the
      launcher's, the provider's APPWIDGET_UPDATE and the countdown's four system broadcasts
      (BOOT_COMPLETED, MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED, TIMEZONE_CHANGED, TIME_SET): a tap on the widget,
      or on the countdown's notification, reaches the activity as an EXPLICIT intent with the
      class id as an extra, and MainActivity turns it into the psync:// link the page already
      understands. Any app can start an exported activity with extras of its own choosing, so
      that id is untrusted: digits only, or it is dropped.

      The activity is portrait only, as the iPhone app is (Info.plist) and as the web manifest
      asks (manifest.json, "portrait-primary"): the page has no landscape layout, and its sheets
      and spend dialogs have never been seen 360px high. configChanges still lists orientation,
      as the Capacitor template wrote it; with the lock it has nothing to do.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"
        tools:targetApi="31">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:exported="true"
            tools:ignore="LockedOrientationActivity,DiscouragedApi">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <!--
          The home-screen widget ("Next class"). exported="false", as Android's own widget
          guide declares a provider: nothing outside the app and the system has any business
          sending to it, and both reach it unexported. APPWIDGET_UPDATE and the other widget
          broadcasts come from the SYSTEM's widget service (a launcher never broadcasts to a
          provider; it asks that service), and the system may send to any receiver; the app's
          own "repaint now" and the repaint alarm's PendingIntent are sent as the app itself.
          Exported, any app could start this process and have it rebuild the widget as often
          as it liked. The provider takes nothing from an intent all the same: whatever
          arrives, it only re-reads the app's own stored snapshot and repaints (or re-arms its
          one alarm), writes nothing, and sends no request. The filter is APPWIDGET_UPDATE
          alone - the app's own refresh and the alarm are explicit intents and need none.
          Not yet seen on a phone: "it PAINTS" in ios-app/ANDROID.md's checklist is its proof.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.NextClassWidgetProvider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="@string/widget_next_class_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/next_class_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <!--
          The class countdown: ONE silent, ongoing notification that counts down to the next
          held class from 90 minutes before it - Android's stand-in for the iPhone's Live
          Activity. This receiver plans it with the app not running: on its own inexact alarm
          (an explicit intent, sent as the app itself), and on four broadcasts that only the
          SYSTEM may send - BOOT_COMPLETED (a restart clears every alarm and every
          notification), MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED (an update of the app removes its notifications),
          and TIMEZONE_CHANGED and TIME_SET (a class's start is read on the phone's own clock,
          so the instant the countdown runs to moves with it). A manifest receiver still
          hears all four (the limits on implicit broadcasts exempt three, and the update's is
          sent to this package alone), and only the first needs a permission. Declared AFTER
          the widget's provider on purpose: tests/suites/19-android-project.js reads the first
          receiver as the provider.

          exported="false": nothing outside the app has any business sending to it, and the
          SYSTEM reaches an unexported receiver with its own broadcasts - the
          local-notifications plugin declares its restore receiver the same way. It takes
          nothing from an intent, whatever arrives: it re-reads the app's own stored snapshot,
          posts, updates or takes down the one notification, and re-arms its one alarm. It
          sends no request, and writes one thing only: it forgets a recorded swipe (below)
          once the plan is about another class.

          NO new permission: RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED and POST_NOTIFICATIONS are declared by
          @capacitor/local-notifications and merged in (the note at the foot of this file).
          There is no service, no exact alarm and no wake lock of the app's own, and the
          notification permission is never asked for from here.
          Not yet seen on a phone: ios-app/ANDROID.md's checklist is its proof.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".countdown.PsyncCountdownReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIMEZONE_CHANGED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIME_SET" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          The countdown's DELETE intent lands here: from Android 14 a member can swipe an
          ongoing notification away, and a swipe is an answer for that class - this receiver
          records which countdown it was (the class's id and its start, in the widget's
          private store, under a name the page cannot write), and no later plan run posts it
          again. NOT exported and with NO intent filter: only the app's own immutable
          PendingIntent, sent by the system, reaches it. It sends no request.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".countdown.PsyncCountdownDismissReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!-- Calendar sync (@ebarooni/capacitor-calendar): its own manifest declares nothing, so both
         are declared here. They are runtime permissions: Android asks the member when the bridge
         first asks the plugin, as iOS does. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CALENDAR" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WRITE_CALENDAR" />

    <!-- Declared by the plugins themselves, and merged in at build time, so NOT repeated here:
         VIBRATE (@capacitor/haptics); POST_NOTIFICATIONS, RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED and WAKE_LOCK
         (@capacitor/local-notifications). The class countdown LEANS on the first two of those
         three: if that plugin is ever removed, or a later version of it stops declaring them,
         declare POST_NOTIFICATIONS and RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED here. -->
</manifest>
